<h1 class="colored">Honesty in tournaments</h1>

<p><i><a href="/RAS/pco627.htm">Julian Conrads</a></i>, 
<i>Bernd Irlenbusch</i>, 
<i><a href="/RAS/pri252.htm">Rainer Rilke</a></i>, 
<i>Anne Schielke</i> and 
<i><a href="/RAS/pwa208.htm">Gari Walkowitz</a></i> (<script language='JavaScript' type='text/javascript'>Obfuscate( '&#116;&#117;&#109;&#46;&#100;&#101;', '&#103;&#97;&#114;&#105;&#46;&#119;&#97;&#108;&#107;&#111;&#119;&#105;&#116;&#122;' )</script>)


<p><i><a href='/article/eeeecolet/'>Economics Letters</a></i>, 2014, vol. 123, issue 1, 90-93
<p><b>Abstract:</b>
We apply the die rolling experiment of Fischbacher and Föllmi-Heusi (2013) to a two-player tournament incentive scheme. Our treatments vary the prize spread. The data highlights that honesty is more pronounced when the prize spread is small.
<p><b>Keywords:</b> <a href="/scripts/search.pf?kw=Experiment">Experiment</a>; <a href="/scripts/search.pf?kw=Compensation schemes">Compensation schemes</a>; <a href="/scripts/search.pf?kw=Tournament">Tournament</a>; <a href="/scripts/search.pf?kw=Honesty">Honesty</a> (search for similar items in EconPapers)<br>
